**Situation.** RoleMirror’s discover page fetched all 4,966 Greenhouse boards inside the Next.js server and kept every description in memory until the last board returned. That process peaked at 8.4 GB and still held about 2.5 GB after the fetch, for the job list and the search index. A Vercel function stops at 2 GB on Hobby and 4 GB on Pro, and it has no disk that keeps `.cache` for the next request. In production the instance was killed while the download was still running, and the browser only showed `Connection closed`.

**Objective.** Run RoleMirror on Vercel, with the open-roles page able to list and search Software jobs without downloading the catalog during the request.

**Action.** The catalog moved to Convex, starting with Software. A sync downloads each board list without the posting body, compares `updated_at` with the stored timestamp, and writes a description only when the job is new or changed. Listings and description text are separate documents, so a timestamp check never loads the body. `/discover` asks Convex for 20 listings at a time. Title and description search use Convex search indexes, and the description text stays in Convex. GitHub Actions runs that sync every six hours.

**Result.** The first sync stored 32,583 Software roles. `/discover` renders one page from Convex, moves to the next page, and a search for “react” returns matches without pulling the catalog into the server. The page no longer depends on an 8 GB process, so it fits a Vercel function.
